Subject: Region scroll animations draw a frame once the content has moved a pixel

0005 eases a region scroll (Claude Code's full-screen view, less, an editor) with a 45 ms time
constant and draws a frame at every refresh until the content is under half a pixel from rest.
For the second half of the way it moves less than a pixel a frame: at 120 Hz a 3-row scroll
(102 px) draws 29 frames, and in the last 14 of them the content moves under a pixel each, frames
that look alike yet each cost a draw, a present and a pass of the compositor, with the display
link kept running.

A region scroll's next frame is now due once its content has moved `region_anim_step_px` (1 px)
since the last drawn frame, or when it comes to rest if that is sooner (`regionAnimStepSeconds`),
and a refresh with nothing due draws nothing (`animationFrameDue`). While frames are due at every
refresh the display link runs as before; once they come further apart it stops
(`animationWantsVsync`), and the renderer thread's animation timer draws each frame when it is
due. The curve is the same: each frame shows the content where the ease has it at that moment,
and the scroll comes to rest when it did before. At 120 Hz the 3-row scroll draws 20 frames
instead of 29 (the Zig test's simulation), the longest gap 50 ms, before the last pixel. A scroll
that joins one in flight starts its ease when it comes, not at the last drawn frame, which in the
tail can be 50 ms ago (`catchUpRegionAnims`).

Each drawn frame also asked for the next: 0005's tick rebuilds the region uniforms, which sets
`cells_rebuilt`, after the draw has cleared it, so while a region animated every refresh redrew
and the display link never paused. The tick now leaves the flag as the draw left it; the uniforms
and ghost rows it rebuilds go into the frame being drawn.

Measured in Calm (headless Debug app, M5 Pro, 2026-09-30, the engine with and without this patch
interleaved, three rounds): a recording of Claude Code streaming an answer (full-screen view, 77
one-row scrolls in 36 s) presented 817-819 frames in 20 s before and 639-658 after, and Calm used
84-90 million instructions a second before and 65-74 after (-20%), with 435-514 interrupt wakeups
a second before and 321-350 after. The frames that differ from the one before stay about as
many: 494-496 before, 482-487 after.

Calm's own patch (not from thdxg/ghostty); applies on top of 0005–0015.
Zig tests: `zig build test -Dtest-filter=regionAnimStepSeconds`.

